This session helped me understand Product Management more from a practical point of view.
One thing that stayed with me was the idea that a product is not really “finished” after it is launched.
It is more like a continuous learning loop where we keep improving based on what we learn.
I could relate this to automation projects, where the first version rarely works exactly as we initially imagined.
Once users start using it, we discover new requirements, gaps and sometimes completely different expectations.

The discussion around accountability was also interesting for me.
A PM can take help from engineering, legal, security or consultants, but the final product decision still belongs to the PM.
This made me realise that getting expert help does not mean transferring accountability.

Another interesting point was that AI products make this even more challenging because AI itself keeps evolving.
A solution or model that looks suitable today may not necessarily be the best option a few months later.
So, an AI Product Manager has to continuously learn and reassess decisions.
I also understood that customer usage and business outcomes should be checked after deployment, not just delivery.
This is something I want to apply more consciously in my own automation work.
Overall, this session made Product Management feel less like a project delivery role and more like managing a living product.

My biggest takeaway is that launching the product is not the end of the PM's responsibility; it is the beginning of the next learning cycle
